  "response": "Attribution in a collaborative-robot incident of this kind rests on two evidentiary strands developed in parallel: the physical behaviour of the cell under controlled re-enactment, and the machine-generated record of who configured it and when. Neither the manufacturer's account nor the plant's is taken at face value; each is tested against data that neither party controls. The dispute between vendor and operator is resolved, in practice, by showing which link in the protective chain departed from its specified state. The injured worker sustained a crush injury to the left hand, and the force metrology on the cell was undertaken only after that injury.\n\nISO/TS 15066 specifies biomechanical force and pressure limits for human-robot contact. A single maximum permissible contact force of 500 newtons for all body regions is fixed in that document. Investigators measured the cell's contact forces using a biofidelic force-pressure measurement device, and those measurements show that the contact force exceeded the ISO/TS 15066 limit for the hand region. The force recorded at the point of contact was above the standard's permissible threshold for hand contact, which fixes the overforce as a matter of measurement rather than of testimony.\n\nThe documentary strand runs alongside the metrological one, and it is assembled from the cell's own memory. Engineers downloaded the robot controller's event log to reconstruct the seconds preceding the contact. Teach pendant access records identified which user profile last modified the cell's speed limit. The overforce established at the point of contact is itself evidence that the force-limiting sensor's output had drifted outside its specified tolerance band. Those same teach pendant access records show that the cell's safety configuration parameters were altered from the values set at commissioning; the overforce traces to the drifted sensor output, to the altered parameters, or to both together.\n\nThe remaining question concerns the state of the protective function itself at the instant of the crush. By contrast with the drifted sensor output, the safety-rated force limiting function was active at the moment of contact. Either that function was active when the worker's hand was crushed or it was disabled: one of the two obtained, and not both. The departure from the commissioned parameter values caused the safety-rated force limiting function to be disabled at the moment of contact, which places the decisive act inside the plant's configuration practice.\n\nISO 13849-1 defines performance levels for safety-related parts of control systems. Recalibration of force-limiting sensors once every five years is required by that standard. Against that normative yardstick, the measured forces, the sensor's drift record and the authenticated configuration history together allocate responsibility between the machine's protective hardware and the plant's handling of the safety envelope, and they do so on the strength of instrumented data rather than either party's narrative of the event.",
